efit from J3.\n\nFinal decision: keep T6 (best draw potential), discard J3 (least useful, honor, but safe).\n\nAnswer: Play J3\n\nBut wait \u2014 valid actions include **Play J3**, yes.\n\nAlso, I have **Play T6** as option \u2014 but I want to keep T6.\n\nSo discard J3.\n\nReason: Maximizes future potential by retaining T6, which has the highest number of unshown copies (3), increasing the chance to draw a pair. J3 is less valuable and safe to discard given no observed interest in dragon melds.\n\nEvaluation: Among the options, keeping T6 offers the best opportunity to form a pair due to its higher remaining count in the wall. Discarding J3 is safe as no player has shown interest in dragon tiles, and it contributes nothing to my current scoring patterns beyond a potential single Dragon Pung (which isn't active yet). Other discards like W9 or T5 are also safe but offer no advantage over J3.\n\nReason: Retaining T6 maximizes the chance of drawing a useful tile, while discarding J3 poses minimal risk given the lack of dragon-related melds by opponents.\n\nAnswer: Play J3"}
